implementationPlan.description:
THE FIX. Edit packages/moe-daemon/src/index.ts only.

Add a private helper right after isForeignDaemonInfo. Do not export it:

  function namesOwnProject(recorded: string, own: string): boolean {
    if (isSameProjectPath(recorded, own)) return true;
    if (isForeignDaemonInfo({ projectPath: recorded })) return false;
    try {
      return isSameProjectPath(fs.realpathSync.native(recorded), fs.realpathSync.native(own));
    } catch {
      return false;
    }
  }

Give it a short why-comment of at most about 5 lines covering these points:
- path.resolve is lexical.
- A path written on this side of the WSL boundary is also compared on disk. realpathSync.native resolves symlinks, junctions and 8.3 names; plain realpathSync leaves 8.3 names unexpanded, so it must be .native.
- A foreign path is compared as written. Resolving it here would read the other OS's /home/u/p as C:\home\u\p, and //x would resolve to /x.
- Any fs error means the path is not ours.

Error handling: the try/catch is the only new failure path. Every realpath error (ENOENT, EACCES, ELOOP, EISDIR on odd Windows drives) returns false, which keeps today's 'projectPath mismatch' behavior. Nothing new throws out of the callers.

Switch exactly these four comparisons from isSameProjectPath to namesOwnProject, keeping `own` as it is computed today:
(a) staleLockReason's 'projectPath mismatch' check, which stays after the unchanged foreign-lock early return;
(b) the precheck in validateDaemonInfoForProject;
(c) the info.projectPath precheck in probeDaemonHealth;
(d) the /health body check in probeDaemonHealth. Keep `typeof parsed.projectPath === 'string'` before it.

Refresh the why-comments at (a) and (b), one line each.

Leave these untouched:
- comparableProjectPath, isSameProjectPath, canonicalProjectPath and isForeignDaemonInfo;
- the export list and acquireLock, which still records path.resolve;
- cleanup policy and TTLs;
- every file and wire shape.

Afterwards, `grep -n 'isSameProjectPath(' packages/moe-daemon/src/index.ts` shows only the function's own definition and the helper's two calls.

GREEN: rerun index.test.ts in the snapshot. X16-X18 and X1-X15 pass.

ADVERSARIAL SELF-REVIEW, once, as the final step. Load adversarial-self-review. Check:
- Foreign data never reaches realpath: X13 still asserts the //x (POSIX) and /x (win32) aliases are false. A lexical match never touches the filesystem.
- Nothing is wrongly judged ours. All of these stay a mismatch:
  - another directory, or a link to it;
  - a missing path (c83d7847's 'moved' control);
  - an unreadable path;
  - a relative recorded path, which is resolved against our cwd, as today.
- Error handling: a realpath ENOENT, EACCES, ELOOP or EISDIR gives false. Nothing escapes staleLockReason, validateDaemonInfoForProject or probeDaemonHealth, and probeDaemonHealth still resolves to a boolean.
- Cost: realpath runs only after a lexical mismatch. A recorded UNC path on an unreachable server can stall it for the SMB timeout. Note this; add no timers.
- Walk every destructive consumer with link, junction and 8.3 spellings in both directions:
  - start blocks without deleting anything;
  - status reports running;
  - stop signals only the validated pid;
  - startDaemon's lock-wait path never cleans up a live daemon;
  - a contender never takes a live lock.
- State in the summary what is out of scope:
  - trailing-dot spellings: Node prefixes win32 fs paths with \\?\, so `<dir>.` is ENOENT and nothing is read or deleted (planner probe);
  - bind mounts: realpath does not see them, so they behave as today;
  - doctor and proxy own-path checks: task-f33bf7af;
  - the empty-lock window: task-4ca62c89.
- Test hygiene: links are unlinked before testDir is removed, X17's child is killed and awaited, the live .moe is never touched, and the cwd is restored.
- Size: this task's own diff stays at or under about 150 net LOC across the two files.

If anything changes, rerun step 3's exact command and submit that output with complete_task.

executionContract.intendedBehavior:
Sometimes daemon.json, daemon.lock or the /health reply names our project directory through a different on-disk spelling: a symlink, a Windows junction or an 8.3 short name, in either direction. The daemon CLI then treats it as our own project:
- start is blocked as 'already running' after one /health probe, and nothing is deleted;
- status reports running;
- stop signals only the validated pid;
- a live lock refuses a contender.
A path recorded for a different directory is still a 'projectPath mismatch' and is cleaned up as before. A path from the other side of the WSL boundary (foreign) is still compared as written.

planningNotes.approachesConsidered:
1) The task's own suggestion, used alone: never delete a native mismatch whose pid is alive and whose /health answers with the recorded path and pid. Rejected as the whole fix, for three reasons:
- a .moe copied from a live project would then block start (exit 1) until someone deletes its daemon.json by hand;
- status and stop would still be wrong for real aliases;
- the lock would stay lexical.

2) Realpath only our own --project, inside canonicalProjectPath. Rejected:
- the daemon records --project raw, so with recorded=symlink and caller=canonical it would still delete;
- it would change what acquireLock writes into the lock.

3) stat dev+ino. Rejected: ReFS/Dev Drive does not keep 64-bit file IDs unique, so it could give a false 'same', and on stop that is destructive.

Chosen: namesOwnProject, which tries the lexical comparison first, then realpathSync.native on both sides for native paths only. It lives at the four lifecycle comparison points, so start, stop, status, the lock-wait path and the lock are all fixed at once.
